    "content": {
      "schemaVersion": 1,
      "format": "MARKDOWN",
      "markdown": "# Vector routes — an AIY demonstration\n\n## A displacement [00:00](#t=0)\n\nStart at the origin of a coordinate grid. Moving three units to the right and one unit up gives the displacement (3, 1). The arrow records the change in position.\n\n![A displacement](assets/vector-owned-1.svg)\n\n## Horizontal and vertical steps [00:20](#t=20)\n\nThe same move can be split into two steps: (3, 0) followed by (0, 1). These components let us record a movement with two numbers in a fixed order.\n\n![Horizontal and vertical steps](assets/vector-owned-2.svg)\n\n## A second displacement [00:40](#t=40)\n\nNow consider the displacement (1, 2). Its horizontal component is one and its vertical component is two. It points in a different direction from (3, 1).\n\n![A second displacement](assets/vector-owned-3.svg)\n\n## Adding displacements [01:00](#t=60)\n\nTake the move (3, 1), then take (1, 2). Adding the components gives (4, 3). The arrow from the original start to the final destination represents the combined move.\n\n![Adding displacements](assets/vector-owned-4.svg)\n\n## Scaling a displacement [01:20](#t=80)\n\nScaling (1, 2) by two gives (2, 4). Both components double, so the direction stays the same while the distance doubles.\n\n![Scaling a displacement](assets/vector-owned-5.svg)\n\n## A route on the plane [01:40](#t=100)\n\nA route can be built from several displacements. The sum records where we finish relative to where we started, even if we choose different intermediate stops.\n\n![A route on the plane](assets/vector-owned-6.svg)\n",
      "transcriptBasis": "NONE",
